<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Sdk</RootNamespace>
        <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
        <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>

        <!--
            A package of MSBuild logic and nothing else. There is no assembly to ship, so there is no
            assembly to reference: IncludeBuildOutput=false keeps lib/ out of the package, and
            NU5128 is the warning that a package with no lib/ has a dependency group it cannot
            match — which is exactly the shape a props/targets package has.
        -->
        <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
        <SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>true</SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128</NoWarn>
        <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>

        <!--
            No symbols, against the repository-wide default. There is no assembly in this package to
            debug into, so the symbol package would be empty — and an empty one is not a harmless
            extra file: `dotnet pack` fails it with NU5017, after having successfully written the
            real package, which reads as a broken build.
        -->
        <IncludeSymbols>false</IncludeSymbols>

        <Description>
            MSBuild integration for Vixen: `dotnet build` imports the project's assets and builds its
            content, and what the importers said arrives as MSBuild diagnostics.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>msbuild;sdk;content;addressables;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
        <PackageType>MSBuildSdk</PackageType>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
        ==============================================================================================
        Where the CLI this package ships is built.

        A property rather than a literal so that a pack can be pointed at an output directory it did
        not produce — which is how the packed layout is checked without rebuilding the whole engine
        first, and how a CI job that builds and packs in two steps names the first step's output.
        ==============================================================================================
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <VixenCliOutputDirectory Condition="'$(VixenCliOutputDirectory)' == ''">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../Vixen.Cli/bin/$(Configuration)/net10.0/</VixenCliOutputDirectory>
        <VixenCliIsBuilt>false</VixenCliIsBuilt>
        <VixenCliIsBuilt Condition="Exists('$(VixenCliOutputDirectory)vixen.dll')">true</VixenCliIsBuilt>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />

        <!--
            Two entry points for two ways of consuming it. `Sdk/` is what `<Project Sdk="Vixen.Sdk">`
            imports; `build/` is what a plain PackageReference imports. Both land on the same two
            files, so there is one implementation and no way for the two forms to drift.
        -->
        <None Include="Sdk\Sdk.props" Pack="true" PackagePath="Sdk\" />
        <None Include="Sdk\Sdk.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="Sdk\" />
        <None Include="build\Vixen.Sdk.props" Pack="true" PackagePath="build\" />
        <None Include="build\Vixen.Sdk.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="build\" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        ==============================================================================================
        The CLI, inside the package, which is what makes `dotnet build` work with nothing installed.

        `build/Vixen.Sdk.targets` has looked for `../tools/vixen.dll` beside itself since it was
        written and nothing ever put one there, so every consumer fell through to the third rung —
        `dotnet vixen`, a tool they had to restore or install themselves. Doc 08's "restores the Vixen
        tool versions matching the referenced packages" was not met, and the SDK and the tool could
        drift: an SDK and a tool that can drift apart will, and the failure is a content build that
        half works.

        ⚠ **Inside this package rather than in one it depends on, and that is forced rather than
        preferred.** `<Project Sdk="Vixen.Sdk/x.y.z">` is resolved by the MSBuild SDK resolver, which
        downloads exactly the one package and never walks its dependency graph — so a `Vixen.Cli.Tools`
        package next door would be restored for the `PackageReference` form and silently absent for the
        `Sdk` form, which is the form the templates scaffold. One package is the only arrangement in
        which both forms get the same tool, and it is also the only one in which the version cannot be
        wrong: it is the same `.nupkg`.

        ⚠ **Packed by path, with a condition, and the consequence is stated rather than hidden:**
        `dotnet pack` on this project alone produces a package with an empty `tools/`, and the targets
        then fall through to `dotnet vixen` exactly as they do today. Packing has to follow a solution
        build. This is the same bargain `Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities` makes with `Vixen.StyleGen`, and
        `Build.CheckCliIsShippable` is what turns "the pack ran too early" into a failed gate rather
        than into a package that is quietly missing its tool.

        ⚠ **Portable, and no `-r`.** A framework-dependent build carries every RID's natives under
        `runtimes/` and picks one at run time from its own `.deps.json`, so one copy serves win-x64,
        win-arm64, linux-x64, linux-arm64, linux-musl (an Alpine CI container), osx-x64 and osx-arm64
        alike. Three RID-specific copies would be smaller per RID and would leave every RID not in the
        list with no tool at all — and the list would be a second hand-maintained thing to be wrong.
        What it costs is that each of the three desktop RIDs downloads all of them: ~170 MB
        uncompressed, of which the RID actually running uses about 30 MB. Assimp and HarfBuzz across
        twenty RIDs are most of it.

        ⚠ **Named by extension, and the extension-less files are excluded on purpose.** `vixen`,
        `vixen-content-server` and `Vixen.AssetCompiler` beside the assemblies are *apphosts* — native
        executables built for whichever machine ran the build — so packing `**/*` would put a Mach-O
        binary called `vixen` into the package a Windows consumer restores. Nothing needs them: the
        targets run `dotnet "…/vixen.dll"`, and `CompilerPool` starts its workers as
        `dotnet "…/Vixen.AssetCompiler.dll"` for the same reason.

        ⚠ **`.pdb` and `.xml` are dropped**, which is 67 MB of native symbols and 15 MB of doc
        comments for an assembly nobody debugs or reads IntelliSense from here.
        ==============================================================================================
    -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(VixenCliIsBuilt)' == 'true'">
        <None Include="$(VixenCliOutputDirectory)*.dll" Pack="true" PackagePath="tools/" Visible="false" />
        <None Include="$(VixenCliOutputDirectory)*.deps.json" Pack="true" PackagePath="tools/" Visible="false" />
        <None Include="$(VixenCliOutputDirectory)*.runtimeconfig.json" Pack="true" PackagePath="tools/" Visible="false" />

        <!--
            ⚠ One directory and no `%(RecursiveDir)`, for the reason `Tools/Vixen.Templates` records:
            NuGet appends an item's own recursive path to a PackagePath that has no extension on it, so
            spelling the path out gives every file its directory twice.
        -->
        <!--
            ⚠ **Assimp's major 6 is dropped and its major 5 is kept, which is the opposite way round
            from how the saving was first filed.** `Ultz.Native.Assimp` 6.0.2 ships two sonames in
            every Linux and macOS RID and the binding names exactly one of them:
            `Silk.NET.Assimp` 2.23.0's `AssimpLibraryNameContainer` reads `libassimp.so.5` on Linux
            and Android, `libassimp.5.dylib` on macOS, and the unversioned `Assimp64.dll` /
            `Assimp32.dll` on Windows — which is why `ci.yml` installs Ubuntu's `libassimp5` and says
            in as many words that a 6 there "would load and then be wrong in ways a signature cannot
            catch". So the copy nothing opens is the **6**, 44 251 540 bytes of it across the five
            RIDs that carry a pair, and Windows has no pair to choose between.

            The version is named rather than globbed. `libassimp.so.*` would drop both the day the
            binding moves to 6 and leave a package whose model importer throws on first use — the one
            failure mode this exclusion can produce, and the reason it is spelt with the number in it.
        -->
        <None Include="$(VixenCliOutputDirectory)runtimes/**/*"
              Exclude="$(VixenCliOutputDirectory)runtimes/**/*.pdb;
                       $(VixenCliOutputDirectory)runtimes/**/libassimp.so.6;
                       $(VixenCliOutputDirectory)runtimes/**/libassimp.6.dylib"
              Pack="true"
              PackagePath="tools/runtimes/"
              Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
